\chapter{Deriving Visitors from AST Schemas}

A visitor combines the vocabulary discovered in an AST schema with user-supplied semantic handlers. Template instantiation generates handler dispatch; the schema determines which tags are accepted.

\section{Register operations with \texttt{o\allowbreak{}n}}

\texttt{o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} in C++ and \texttt{o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} in D produce a \texttt{V\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}. Handlers may take one node argument or a compile-time tag followed by a node. The node carries runtime values; the tag identifies the handler specialization.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto number = dsl::on<"num">([](auto tag, const dsl::ASTNode& node) {
    static_assert(tag.value == "num");
    assert(!node.value().empty());
});
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct NumberHandler
{
    void opCall(Tag)(Tag tag, ref ASTNode node)
    {
        static assert(Tag.value == "num");
        assert(node.value().length != 0);
    }
}
auto number = on!"num"(NumberHandler());
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

D also accepts alias-bound generic handlers through \texttt{o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. Concrete functions/\allowbreak{}delegates/\allowbreak{}functors retain their types in the visitor.

\section{Derive from a runtime AST}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto schema = dsl::node<"add">(dsl::leaf<"num">(0));
int sum = 0;
auto visitor = dsl::derive_visitor(schema,
    dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
    dsl::on<"num">([&](const dsl::ASTNode& n) { sum += std::stoi(n.value()); }));
auto tree = dsl::node<"add">(dsl::leaf<"num">(2), dsl::leaf<"num">(3));
visitor.walk(tree);
assert(sum == 5);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import std.conv : to;
auto schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
int sum;
auto visitor = derive_visitor(schema,
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) { sum += to!int(n.value()); }));
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(2), leaf!"num"(3));
visitor.walk(tree);
assert(sum == 5);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Schema collection discovers unique tags recursively. Its values and arities do not constrain later trees: the schema above permits an \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d} with two \texttt{n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m} children although its example tree has only one. The validation contract is tag coverage. The user handler can enforce additional structural rules.

Every schema tag must have a handler at construction. An unknown tag in a visited tree raises an exception. Handlers execute in registration order, with the first matching case used. \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})} also offers this runtime-validated construction.

\section{Validate the schema at compilation}

C++ supplies a structural tree of types:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto schema = dsl::ast_schema<"add">(dsl::ast_schema<"num">());
auto visitor = dsl::compile_visitor(schema,
    dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
    dsl::on<"num">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}>} records the tag and child types. \texttt{a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a} constructs its type-level vocabulary, and \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r} checks handler coverage with a compile-time assertion before creating the runtime visitor.

D can use an \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} value computed by CTFE:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
enum schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) {}));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Each discovered schema tag is checked by \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t}. The handler code is still supplied by the user. A runtime schema selects accepted tags and validates them at runtime; a static schema validates them during host-language compilation. Neither path performs runtime JIT compilation.

\section{Dispatch and traversal}

\texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})} dispatches one node. \texttt{w\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{})} recursively traverses:

\begin{xltabular}{\linewidth}{|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X|}
\hline
\textbf{Order} & \textbf{C++} & \textbf{D} & \textbf{Handler timing} \\ \hline
Preorder & \texttt{V\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} & \texttt{V\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} & Before children \\ \hline
Postorder & \texttt{V\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} & \texttt{V\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} & After children \\ \hline
\end{xltabular}

Preorder is the default. C++ traversal supports const or mutable ASTs when the handlers accept the corresponding node reference. D's public visitor operations take \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}, permitting mutation by callbacks. Handlers that change child structure affect the traversal as it runs; choose the order to suit the transformation.

\section{Equality-triggered visitor operations}

After saturation, convert an extracted representative to \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} and dispatch it through a visitor:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto graph = new EGraph;
auto root = graph.add("(add a 0)");
auto atom = graph.add("a");
assert(graph.saturate(sexpr_rules("(= (add ?x 0) ?x)")).saturated());
size_t calls;
graph.when_equal(root, atom, (EGraph g, EClassId id) {
    auto tree = g.extract_ast(id);
    enum schema = leaf!"atom"("");
    auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
        on!"atom"((ref ASTNode n) { ++calls; assert(n.value() == "a"); }));
    visitor.walk(tree);
});
assert(calls == 1);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The C++ equivalent is in \href{../examples/26-equality-saturation.cpp}{\texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}6\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}}. Grammar-produced ASTs use nonterminal names and \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n} leaves, so the same visitor mechanism can implement grammar semantic passes (Chapter 36).
